<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for FlashTeX.app when signed with a Developer ID identity and
  the hardened runtime (`make-app.sh - -sign <identity>`; passed to codesign as
  `- -entitlements` for the app bundle only). The bundled Rust helpers in
  Contents/MacOS are signed with the hardened runtime and NO entitlements: they
  are plain, statically linked executables (otool -L lists only /usr/lib and
  /System frameworks) that read stdin, write stdout/files, and never load
  plug-ins, JIT, or debug anything.

  The dictionary below is deliberately EMPTY. Every entitlement the hardened
  runtime or notarization could plausibly call for was checked against what
  the app actually does (apps/mac/Sources/FlashTeXMac, revision this file was
  committed with) and rejected:

  * com.apple.security.app-sandbox — not requested. Notarization requires the
    hardened runtime, not the App Sandbox; the app opens arbitrary user .tex
    projects, spawns helpers from Contents/MacOS and writes ledgers under
    ~/Library/Application Support/FlashTeX. Adopting the sandbox is a separate
    product decision (Mac App Store), not a packaging one.

  * com.apple.security.network.server / .network.client — sandbox-only keys;
    they have no effect without app-sandbox. The Nearby listener
    (NearbyListener.swift: NWListener over TLS 1.2 + PSK, Bonjour service
    _flashtex._tcp, optionally loopback-only) and the loopback TLS companion
    client need no entitlement under the hardened runtime. What they DO need
    is Info.plist metadata, which make-app.sh already writes:
    NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription (otherwise macOS 14+ silently denies the
    local-network prompt) and NSBonjourServices listing _flashtex._tcp.

  * com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation — not needed. The app
    links only system frameworks and the Swift runtime shipped with macOS 14;
    it loads no third-party dylibs, bundles, or plug-ins (no dlopen /
    Bundle(path:) in the sources). The helpers are separate processes, started
    with Foundation.Process; spawning a child never requires this key.

  * com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory,
    com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit — no JIT, no writable+executable pages.

  * com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables — the app reads
    FLASHTEX_* environment variables (FLASHTEX_LOG, FLASHTEX_AUTOATTACH,
    FLASHTEX_NO_ACTIVATE, helper overrides); those are ordinary variables, not
    DYLD_*. Nothing injects libraries into the app or the helpers.

  * com.apple.security.cs.debugger, com.apple.security.get-task-allow — the
    app debugs nothing, and get-task-allow makes notarization reject the
    submission; debugging a signed build is done with an ad-hoc build instead.

  * com.apple.security.automation.apple-events — the app sends no Apple
    Events (no NSAppleScript / NSAppleEventDescriptor in the sources). It only
    RECEIVES the standard quit event from launch-check.sh / the user, which
    needs nothing on the receiving side.

  * com.apple.security.device.camera / .audio-input, personal-information.*,
    files.user-selected.* , files.bookmarks.* — no camera, microphone,
    contacts, calendar, photos or location use; file access outside a sandbox
    is ordinary POSIX access.

  If a future change needs one of these (for example the Nearby transport
  moving into an XPC service, or a plug-in loader), add the key here with the
  reason next to it, and re-run `make-app.sh - -sign` so the new entitlement is
  visible in `codesign -d - -entitlements -` output.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict/>
</plist>
